WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.423
Nine days on

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.543
Ubuntu still had

00:00:02.355 --> 00:00:03.262
no fix

00:00:03.798 --> 00:00:05.248
On November 16th

00:00:05.319 --> 00:00:07.194
details of NoisyTCP

00:00:07.383 --> 00:00:08.691
a remote memory

00:00:08.503 --> 00:00:09.670
corruption flaw in

00:00:09.482 --> 00:00:10.649
the Linux kernel's

00:00:10.473 --> 00:00:11.651
TCP stack

00:00:11.687 --> 00:00:12.689
were published

00:00:12.828 --> 00:00:13.983
Nine days later

00:00:14.019 --> 00:00:15.186
Ubuntu had not

00:00:14.998 --> 00:00:16.177
published a fix

00:00:16.702 --> 00:00:17.857
Other distributions

00:00:17.669 --> 00:00:18.400
had a fix

00:00:18.211 --> 00:00:18.966
ready when the

00:00:18.777 --> 00:00:20.180
details were published

00:00:20.275 --> 00:00:21.336
and a KVM

00:00:21.148 --> 00:00:22.598
host escape affecting

00:00:22.421 --> 00:00:23.895
AMD virtualization

00:00:24.061 --> 00:00:25.782
disclosed November 18th

00:00:25.877 --> 00:00:26.937
had no Ubuntu

00:00:26.749 --> 00:00:27.444
fix

00:00:27.974 --> 00:00:29.117
The author argues

00:00:28.929 --> 00:00:30.344
remote kernel flaws

00:00:30.179 --> 00:00:31.323
need updates within

00:00:31.134 --> 00:00:31.747
hours

00:00:31.842 --> 00:00:32.867
not days or

00:00:32.679 --> 00:00:33.705
weeks and notes

00:00:33.528 --> 00:00:35.238
Ubuntu backports patches

00:00:35.049 --> 00:00:35.863
to an older

00:00:35.674 --> 00:00:36.771
kernel instead of

00:00:36.583 --> 00:00:37.761
tracking long-term

00:00:37.573 --> 00:00:38.422
releases

00:00:38.958 --> 00:00:39.972
Full story on

00:00:39.783 --> 00:00:40.679
the dashboard
